1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:03,440
A mastodon left a trace in northern Greenland.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:04,550
Not a bone.

3
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:05,700
Not a tusk.

4
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:06,830
DNA.

5
00:00:07,170 --> 00:00:12,340
Broken pieces of genetic material, recovered from sediment roughly two million

6
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:13,090
years old.

7
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:16,370
Look at that place today: a polar desert.

8
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:20,230
Now try to fit an elephant-like animal into the picture.

9
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:21,980
Something is missing.

10
00:00:22,330 --> 00:00:23,580
Not just the animal.

11
00:00:24,010 --> 00:00:25,380
The world around it.

12
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:32,880
In 2022, scientists reported evidence of a mastodon lineage from Greenland's Kap Copenhagen Formation.

13
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:36,580
Plant remains had already hinted at a greener past.

14
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:40,700
But the dirt held another way into that vanished landscape.

15
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:43,250
And the giant was only the beginning.

16
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:46,050
Because finding a trace is one thing.

17
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:49,730
Working out what left it, how it survived, and what sort

18
00:00:49,730 --> 00:00:52,820
of world it belonged to is a very different puzzle.

19
00:00:53,483 --> 00:00:56,083
An animal does not have to die in front of you

20
00:00:56,283 --> 00:00:57,383
to leave evidence.

21
00:00:57,793 --> 00:01:00,743
Living things shed biological material.
